SuperMario comparison follow-ups
================================

Run bash system/tests/check-toolbox.sh toolbox-additions from WSL. With no
arguments it runs Linux ASan/UBSan alias/date parser checks and Windows x64
fixtures for the actual Roman script, international comparison/table, alias
update, partial-resource and date/time trap entry points. Individual Windows
fixtures can be selected by name, for example date_api or partial_resource.

The independent reference is the user's local SuperMarioProj.1994-02-09:
Toolbox/ScriptMgr/InternationalPACK.a, ScriptMgrExtensions.a,
ScriptMgrUtilDate.a, Toolbox/AliasMgr/alExt.c, and
Toolbox/ResourceMgr/ResourceMgrExtensions.a. ResourceMgrExtensions.a explicitly
lists resident-resource handling as unfinished work; those comments are not
an implemented reference path. ROMAW edits resident bytes and persists
through its existing resource writer instead.

The bounded alias decoder checks version-2 record/tag extents, Pascal names,
parent traversal and truncated records. Windows update tests exercise lookup
and allocation failures, unchanged targets, handle state and preservation of
application-owned user type/data. NewAlias and UpdateAlias build complete path
metadata with ancestor IDs and relative levels; NewAliasMinimal stays minimal.
Relative lookup uses the supplied fromFile first, then retries the stored
absolute path. ResolveAlias refreshes a relocated record on success; failure to
allocate that refresh does not lose a successfully resolved target. Finder's
follow call leaves the record untouched and retries the absolute path for
same-folder relative candidates, following SuperMario's Finder-specific rule.
The native creation path is limited to 255-byte Macintosh paths and 128 ancestor
steps; cyclic or invalid catalogs fail explicitly. This does not implement
volume-wide file-ID searches or network mounting.
Minimal aliases now use the catalog parent and target IDs in their respective
version-2 fields and distinguish files from folders. ResolveAlias validates
records, accepts stored full paths and old ROMAW minimal records, and checks
the target against the catalog before publishing its output.

Comparisons without an explicit sorting resource reuse native Roman locale
collation. Explicit itl2 handles execute their Init, Fetch, Project, Vernier and
Exit callbacks through the 68K runtime. IUTextOrder uses the selected Roman
sorting resource when script/language ordering is equal. The guest IUNSortFrame
layout, condition-code-controlled defaults, expansions, ignored characters and
exit-result changes are preserved. Callback cursors must remain in the supplied
input spans; malformed vectors, invalid stack extents and non-progressing hooks
fall back to native collation with a diagnostic. Resources stay locked during
callbacks and the caller's registers and condition codes are restored. Script
and language ordering reads bounded itlm mapping tables when available, with
Roman current/default selectors and script grouping. Missing or malformed maps
use a deterministic fallback. Only Roman is installed/enabled; absent scripts
follow it in code order. Table tests check all five selectors, extents and four-byte guest
handle outputs on a 64-bit host.

DateCacheRec is opaque. The native parser stores a versioned snapshot of Roman
locale names/separators and the current year entirely inside its 512 guest
bytes, with no host pointers. The tests cover numeric date orders, named dates,
Gregorian leap boundaries, AM/PM, 24-hour times, output preservation and 80,000
exact-size random inputs. This is not a replacement for multi-script tokenizer
tables or guest code that accesses Apple's private cache layout directly.

Partial-resource fixtures intercept allocation and persistence boundaries;
they check reads of modified resident data, extending writes, resizing, zero
fill, overlapping input, protected/read-only resources, invalid ranges and
error propagation. They do not establish resource-editor application workflow
compatibility. Run check-toolbox.sh resource-map as an additional check of the
existing resource-map writer's EOF/header handling.

Run system/tests/check-toolbox.ps1 toolbox-resource in PowerShell against the
staged executable for real Resource Manager persistence. It starts a private
Browser process under Windows GDB, uses an isolated preferences profile and
writes only a disposable resource file under build/. It checks resident and
unloaded reads, partial writes, grow/shrink across close/reopen and preservation
of a neighboring resource. It also creates and resolves aliases for a real
file and folder, checking their version-2 catalog fields. ROMAW and Application parameters can override
the defaults. The probe terminates only its own debugger/application process.
The same probe supplies an original itlm fixture through the real Resource
Manager and verifies resource-driven language order and current/default selectors.
Its Bundle and Copy trees verify relative-first resolution when the old target
still exists, automatic record refresh, and no-change repeated resolution.
alias_relative_test.c separately covers missing and wrong-kind candidates,
cross-volume creation, Finder same-folder fallback, cycles and refresh failure.

ResolveAliasFile reads the first alis resource of files carrying the Finder
alias flag, including resources whose ID is not zero. It follows either one
link or up to ten links, checks folders before interpreting their Finder flags,
and leaves the original FSSpec intact on a broken or overlong chain. Resource
files are opened read-only; the selected map and resource-loading state are
restored. Resolution does not rewrite the alias file. alias_file_test.c covers
chain limits, malformed/missing resources, open failures and broken targets;
the staged resource probe verifies a real sidecar-backed alias file. Existing
resolution limits (no volume-wide ID search or network mounting) still apply.

Run check-toolbox.ps1 itl2-sort for original assembled 68000 callbacks under both
the interpreter and x64 JIT. The probe checks actual Pack6 trap dispatch,
selected-resource lookup through the Resource Manager, hook effects on ordering,
frame/register/condition-code preservation and resource lock restoration. It
requires WSL m68k-linux-gnu-as and m68k-linux-gnu-objcopy. No Apple machine code
is bundled in these fixtures. itl2_sort_test.c covers additional mocked callback
cases (multi-byte fetch, ignored text, stalled callbacks and invalid cursors),
and itl2_layout_test.c runs bounded-vector checks under ASan/UBSan.
